1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,743
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 26.

2
00:00:05,363 --> 00:00:09,216
What happens when one is added to an unsigned long that already holds

3
00:00:09,216 --> 00:00:13,293
its largest value, and how can the counting program detect it?

4
00:00:14,993 --> 00:00:18,796
Unsigned arithmetic is modular, like a car odometer.

5
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:22,900
After the largest value, adding one wraps around to zero.

6
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:28,266
That wrap is the signal. Count up, and stop when i plus one equals zero.

7
00:00:28,266 --> 00:00:30,587
At that moment, i is the maximum.

8
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:36,315
The theory: C plus plus does not trap integer overflow.

9
00:00:36,315 --> 00:00:38,504
Unsigned values wrap around.

10
00:00:38,504 --> 00:00:41,319
Signed overflow is undefined behavior.

11
00:00:41,319 --> 00:00:45,384
And floating point overflow saturates at infinity instead.

12
00:00:46,985 --> 00:00:51,372
So it wraps to zero, and the program stops when i plus one equals zero.

13
00:00:52,191 --> 00:00:56,417
Expecting integer overflow to raise an error. It silently wraps.

14
00:00:57,137 --> 00:01:01,958
Exam tip: zero minus one also wraps, the other way, to the maximum.

15
00:01:01,958 --> 00:01:04,313
Both directions show up on quizzes.
